Third-party native components shipped by openzl-jni

The native libraries use OpenZL 0.3.0. The full license texts and applicable
source notices are in META-INF/licenses. The versioned source locations and
SHA-256 values are recorded in META-INF/native-licenses.json.

- OpenZL 0.3.0 — licenses/OpenZL-LICENSE.txt
- Protobuf 32.0 — licenses/Protobuf-LICENSE.txt
- Abseil 20250512.1 — licenses/Abseil-LICENSE.txt
- utf8_range Protobuf 32.0 — licenses/utf8_range-LICENSE.txt
- Zstandard 1.5.7 — licenses/Zstandard-LICENSE.txt
- LZ4 library 1.10.0 — licenses/LZ4-LICENSE.txt
- XGBoost 3.1.0 — licenses/XGBoost-LICENSE.txt
- dmlc-core 8986b0598df709117570984571476c3614f55724 — licenses/dmlc-core-LICENSE.txt
- Ryu (within XGBoost) XGBoost 3.1.0 — licenses/Ryu-NOTICE.txt
- xxHash (within OpenZL) OpenZL 0.3.0 — licenses/xxHash-OpenZL-LICENSE.txt
- xxHash (within Zstandard) Zstandard 1.5.7 — licenses/xxHash-Zstandard-LICENSE.txt
- pdqsort (modified by OpenZL) OpenZL 0.3.0 — licenses/pdqsort-LICENSE.txt
- FSE/HUF (within OpenZL) OpenZL 0.3.0 — licenses/FSE-HUF-NOTICE.txt

- GSL code in XGBoost span — licenses/GSL-LICENSE.txt and licenses/GSL-NOTICE.txt
- ConcurrentQueue code in dmlc-core — licenses/ConcurrentQueue-LICENSE.txt
- sweater code in XGBoost — licenses/sweater-LICENSE.txt and licenses/sweater-NOTICE.txt

Abseil, XGBoost and dmlc-core are used under Apache-2.0. The Ryu code
included by XGBoost is used under its Apache-2.0 option, whose full text
is included as XGBoost-LICENSE.txt. The full Ryu copyright notice is
preserved in Ryu-NOTICE.txt. Zstandard and the FSE/HUF code are used under
their BSD license options. The LZ4 library is used under BSD-2-Clause.

The wrapper's BSD license and attribution are in META-INF/LICENSE and
META-INF/NOTICE.

Windows builds statically link GCC and MinGW runtime components. Their
supplier license documents, including the GCC Runtime Library Exception,
are packaged beside the DLL under lib/windows_amd64/licenses. Their exact
compiler version and file checksums are in lib/windows_amd64/runtime-licenses.json.
These runtime documents apply to the Windows binary. Operating-system
libraries are supplied by the user's system.

Compatibility modifications applied during the native build are described in
THIRD_PARTY.md in the source repository.
